<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolutions of 1989</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Autumn of Nations, Fall of communism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The revolutions of 1989 were the wave of largely peaceful uprisings that overthrew the communist regimes of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. Beginning with negotiated change in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where the trade union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> won partly free elections in June, they spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> ended in bloodshed and the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>. They brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a>, led to the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990 and opened the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It also set off a chain reaction across the Soviet Union’s satellite states, the mostly bloodless <b>Revolutions of 1989</b>, in Polish the Autumn of Nations, which overthrew each Moscow-imposed regime and led in the end to the dissolution of the Soviet Union in the early 1990s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Economic stagnation and Mikhail Gorbachev’s reforms led to the <b>revolutions of 1989</b>, which ended communist rule in Eastern Europe; in March 1990 the Soviet Union amended Article 6 of its constitution, and in December 1991 the Soviet Union itself dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>revolutions of 1989</b> a neoliberal consensus replaced the social democratic one in the advanced capitalist world, and many socialist parties embraced the Third Way.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The song became tied to the <b>Revolutions of 1989</b> and the fall of the Berlin Wall, and the Scorpions played it at the Brandenburg Gate on 9 November 1999, the tenth anniversary.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wind-of-change">Wind of Change</a></li></ul></section>
